Lazy-loading with the router:



- 🔥Lazy loading consists in splitting the application into several JavaScript bundles, by loading child
    routes lazily. The application routes array, instead of defining all the routes of the application,
    only contains the main, eagerly-loaded routes, as well as parent routes with lazy-loaded children,
    which are thus unknown initially.

- 🔥When the user navigates to the path of an unknown child route, then the Angular router loads the
    JavaScript bundle containing the child routes (and their associated components and services), and
    adds the child routes, components and services to the application.

             _______________________________
            | main.bundle.js    -   100KB   |
            |_______________________________|               ____________________________
            | app.routes.ts                 |  --------->   | 0.chunk.js    -   50KB    |
            |_______________________________|               |___________________________|               
                                                            | admin.routes.ts           |
                                                            |___________________________|     

- 📝it’s actually possible to load the lazy-loading bundles in the background, after the main bundle
    has been loaded and the application has started, without waiting for the user to navigate to the
    child routes, thanks to an alternative preloadingStrategy.


- 🔥🔥To illustrate how we can configure lazy loading, we will assume that you want to define an admin
    section in your application, that should be lazy loaded

    1️⃣The first step is to define an admin component, and at least one route for this component in a file
    named admin.routes.ts:

        export const adminRoutes: Routes = [{ path: '', component: Admin }];
    
    2️⃣The final step (yes, that’s all it takes) is to add a parent route in the main app.routes.ts file,
    and tell the router to lazy-load the admin routes when navigating to that route (or any child route
    it might have):

        { path: 'admin', loadChildren: () => import('./admin/admin.routes').then(m => m.adminRoutes) }
    

    (💡Explanation)
        1️⃣ means, inside the admin section, the empty path ('') maps to Admin component but angular
        does nothing with it. it's just an exported array sitting in a file.

        2️⃣ is saying: “When the user navigates to /admin, dynamically load another route configuration
        file, and continue route matching from there.” 🧠🧠So Angular is not rendering a component at
        this level. It is loading more routes.

        🧠🧠After 2️⃣ admin.routes.ts becomes the child route configurations of the admin route. 
        and Angular combines 1 and 2 conceptually like this:

            {
                path: 'admin',
                children: [
                    { path: '', component: Admin }
                ]
            }
        so navigating to /admin (admin + '') finally renders the Admin component


- As you can see, this is achieved by using the loadChildren property of the route definition and the
    dynamic import function from TypeScript.
    💡When building this application, Angular CLI parses the route configurations and detects that the
    admin child module is lazy-loaded. Without any more work on your part, it generates an additional
    JavaScript bundle for the admin module (named 0.chunk.js), and generates the necessary JavaScript
    to load this bundle when the router requires './admin/admin.routes'


- 🔥🔥You can even simplify the import if you use a default export(Chapter1, section 15 Modules) for
    the module:
        
        const defaultAdminRoutes: Routes = [{ path: '', component: Admin }];
        export default defaultAdminRoutes;
    
    🔥🔥Then the router will automatically "unwrap" the import:
        
        { path: 'admin', loadChildren: () => import('./admin/admin.routes') }


- 🔥🔥Finally, if (as above) all you want to do is to lazy-load a single component for a given route,
    then it’s even easier: you don’t even have to define an additional routes file. The component can
    be lazyloaded directly:

        { path: 'admin', loadComponent: () => import('./admin/admin').then(m => m.Admin) }